1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:05,340
Could a dying star explode near our
solar system and rip Earth's atmosphere

2
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,720
away into space?

3
00:00:07,939 --> 00:00:13,260
Do science fiction movies show a fiery
blast wave sweeping our planet bare within

4
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:14,060
seconds?

5
00:00:14,854 --> 00:00:18,360
Why does this question matter when
considering terrestrial life?

6
00:00:19,444 --> 00:00:22,420
How essential is our oxygen
atmosphere for survival?

7
00:00:23,671 --> 00:00:28,560
In reality, a supernova is a powerful
explosion marking the death of a star.

8
00:00:29,338 --> 00:00:31,200
When a massive star runs out of fuel,

9
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,860
its core collapses under
immense gravitational force.

10
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:40,640
Alternatively, an exploding white dwarf
star undergoes runaway nuclear fusion.

11
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:46,580
These stellar detonations expel several
solar masses of matter into the surrounding

12
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:47,800
interstellar medium.

13
00:00:48,585 --> 00:00:53,600
The ejected material travels outward at
speeds reaching several per cent of the

14
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:54,400
speed of light.

15
00:00:54,892 --> 00:00:59,880
This creates an expanding shock wave that
sweeps up a shell of interstellar gas and

16
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:00,680
dust.

17
00:01:01,888 --> 00:01:05,477
Where are the nearest stars that might
explode in our stellar neighbourhood?

18
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:11,440
Astronomers define a near-Earth supernova
as one occurring within roughly 33

19
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:14,039
to 978 light-years.

20
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:19,520
Currently, our galaxy holds up to 14
potential near-Earth supernovas inside

21
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:20,840
that broader distance.

22
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:26,700
A few famous stars located within
several hundred light-years could

23
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,260
explode in just 1,000 years.

24
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:33,420
Although these dying stars
will shine brilliantly,

25
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,040
they pose little direct
threat to our planet.

26
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:40,880
What about the famous red
supergiant star Betelgeuse?

27
00:01:42,007 --> 00:01:47,060
Scientists estimate that Betelgeuse
could require as long as 1.5 million

28
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,260
years to explode as a supernova.

29
00:01:50,024 --> 00:01:54,880
When it detonates, its particle stream
will reach Earth about 100,000 years

30
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:56,440
after the initial light.

31
00:01:57,121 --> 00:02:02,040
Looking back in time, other relatively
nearby stars have exploded in Earth's past.

32
00:02:02,817 --> 00:02:07,560
The Vela supernova exploded about
12,000 years ago roughly 800

33
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:09,320
light-years away from our Sun.

34
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:15,480
Another explosion called Geminga
occurred roughly 300,000 years ago at a

35
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,215
distance of 550 light-years.

36
00:02:18,674 --> 00:02:20,720
The closest known white dwarf candidate,

37
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,000
IK Pegasi, is currently moving
away to a safe distance.
